# Dinchy agent rules

Always-on principles for working in this codebase. Task-specific detail lives in
the skills referenced under **Reference** below; open the relevant one when a task
calls for it. Domain vocabulary lives in `CONTEXT.md`.

## Core principles

* Prioritize correctness and clarity. If a choice trades correctness for speed, choose correctness; if a change introduces ambiguity, prefer the option easier to reason about later.
* Prefer working in existing files and packages unless you are introducing a new logical component. Keep related code close to the feature it serves; avoid new top-level structure unless the existing one cannot hold the change cleanly.
* Avoid drive-by refactors and unrelated cleanup. Keep the change scoped; if cleanup is necessary, do it with clear purpose and a clean end state, not shims or incidental patches.
* Keep comments rare — only for non-obvious intent, a trap, or a constraint not already clear from the code. This governs inline comments, not Go doc comments: every exported symbol and package must still carry a terse, intent-focused Go doc comment (enforced by `revive`).
* Never silently discard errors. If an error is intentionally ignored, make that explicit; if it is not actionable, preserve enough context to explain why.
* Never log and return the same error. Log it if this layer is the end of the line for observability; return it if the caller still needs to decide how to handle it.

## Errors

* Always add context when returning an error, and make that context uniquely identify the code path when multiple returns can fail in the same function.
* Use `internal/foundation/errors.AppError` for user-visible failures and anything crossing a layer boundary. Keep the stable code and localized `i18n` message at the source; annotate (not replace) as it travels up.
* Prefer returning errors all the way up. Only `main` should call `fatal`.
* Detail — constructors, `Annotate`/`WithCause`, `errors.Join`, `%w`/`%q`/`%T`, i18n workflow, typed metadata: skill `error-handling`.

## Logging

* Log each application event once, at the boundary that owns it. Do not log the same event in a callee and its caller.
* Error logging is boundary-only and goes solely through `internal/platform/logging.Error` / `logging.HTTPError`; never call `slog` error methods with an error value outside that package. `AppError` carries a log-once marker, and `go run ./cmd/coderules logreturn ./...` enforces that no function both logs and returns an error (exempt a deliberate dual-failure path with `//dinchy:allow-logreturn <reason>`).
* Detail — `Info`/`Warn` guidance, message style, structured field names: skill `logging`.

## Conventions

* Keep feature-local helpers, handlers, services, and types together under the same feature package when that reduces indirection.
* Define API input validation and normalization at the transport boundary; handlers and services operate on validated, transformed values only.
* All environment variables must come from `internal/config`; do not define env var names ad hoc.
* Tests cover caller-visible behavior (error codes, metadata, responses), not implementation trivia.
* Detail — feature file classes (`*_service.go`, `events.json`, …), the `Resolve` pattern, test and `*_mockdata_test.go` naming: skill `feature-conventions`.
* Detail — godoc package comments, when a `doc.go` is warranted, and testable `Example` functions: skill `godoc`.

## Naming and scope

* Use full words for identifiers and variables; avoid abbreviations unless universally understood and materially clearer.
* Name files after the feature or concern they implement, not their internal mechanics.
* Keep new rules high-signal: add one only when it captures a repeated, non-obvious trap. If a naming pattern or workflow changes, get it approved and record it here before using it.

## Reference

Task-specific detail is kept out of this always-loaded file. Open by path when relevant:

* Domain vocabulary and module ownership map — `CONTEXT.md`
* Error construction, annotation, formatting, i18n — `.agents/skills/error-handling/SKILL.md`
* Logging levels, message style, structured fields — `.agents/skills/logging/SKILL.md`
* Feature file classes, validation, config, test naming — `.agents/skills/feature-conventions/SKILL.md`
* Godoc package comments, `doc.go` vs regular file, testable Examples — `.agents/skills/godoc/SKILL.md`
